WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.109
At Hillsdale, you’re taught how to think.

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:10.310
You’re taught how to meet challenges and
exceed them and wait for the next challenge.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.070
That has helped me my entire career.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:16.460
That chance to just spend four years immersed
in learning and becoming a better human and

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:21.449
to focus on the soul and the body—it’s
irreplaceable.

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:25.369
I got involved in the Collegian very early
and from there it confirmed that I wanted

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:30.510
to get into journalism, so I spent the next
four years—outside of my tenure in Washington

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.020
with WHIP—focused mostly on the Collegian.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.750
Collegian sort-of became the focal point of
debate about what Hillsdale was going to be.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:42.269
Those relationships that were forged have
been lifelong relationships.

00:00:42.269 --> 00:00:46.230
We had people who were just amazingly great
at their fields.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.960
They were brilliant, they were world-renowned
in Political Science and History, and yet

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.840
they wanted to teach; they wanted to be with
us; they want to make us better.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.219
I had no clue where I was going to go.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:58.600
I just wanted to go out and cover stories
and write and then got into editing.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.079
I started at the bottom.

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:01.860
From there, I kind-of got opportunity after
opportunity.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.510
It was just kind-of serendipity.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:09.070
That led to my job at the AP where I was deputy
business editor and then the business editor.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:10.130
They cover the world.

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:14.000
There’s nothing like being at the AP when
a big story breaks.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.290
And then out of the blue I get a phone call.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.360
I met with Delta’s Chief Executive Officer
then and the President—and the President

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.140
is now the CEO—he said, "Listen Kevin,
you’re a great storyteller and we need to

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.409
improve storytelling at Delta.

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:28.770
But most importantly, your values fit ours.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.250
And at Delta, values are the most important
thing and how we treat people."

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:38.290
Two years in, it is the key reason why Delta
is considered the best airline in the world.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:39.509
Did I ever think I’d be here?

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:40.509
No.

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:41.670
But I never thought I really was anywhere.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.270
My career has just sort-of gone from place
to place.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.219
I’ve been blessed.

00:01:48.219 --> 00:01:52.659
Grigor sent me an email and I open it up and
it says "Congratulations, you’re going

00:01:52.659 --> 00:01:53.659
to be awarded."

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:58.540
And I literally laughed because this is the
last thing I ever thought.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.369
I’m undeserving of the award.

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:05.079
I’ve had a good career and just thrilled
and totally honored to get this recognition.

00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:06.840
It means a ton to me.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.280
I can’t sit here and say specifically, "Oh,
I am where I am because of Hillsdale."

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.420
But I am.

